      "text": "To solve this problem, we will use the concept of combinations and the basic probability formula.\n\nFirst, let's determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10. This can be calculated using the combination formula \\( C(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items, \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial.\n\nHere, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\). So, the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10 is:\n\\[\nC(10, 2) = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5. Here, \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\). So, the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5 is:\n\\[\nC(5, 2) = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nThe probability that both chosen members are girls is the number of ways to choose 2 girls divided by the total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10. Therefore, the probability is:\n\\[\n\\frac{C(5, 2)}{C(10, 2)} = \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that the two chosen members are both girls is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\\).",
